{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact head 9f2324dced4e1b92582e7c18bf37347d10339326, tree ab5a59a117db3870a85efcd0949e49e65b021f96, on builder/cv1-formal-pins-20261116, all 9 artifacts, for request ea39a112 under my promise ea126673. This verdict names the one implementation: CV-1 (request 77a00c9d, promise 42d64605, report 524b2ee1, the CV-1 note, primary). Main 9d69f807 is an ancestor, and two commits change nine paths. `git merge-tree --write-tree 957b2755 9f2324dc` merges cleanly onto the current main.\n\nWhat I read:\n\n- `scripts/check-formal-source-hashes.mjs`. Without arguments it reads `coverage.json`, hashes each mapped file and fails with the file, the model and one refresh command for each mismatch. It starts no subprocess, imports no product code and writes nothing. `--refresh Model` reads HEAD from Git, refuses before any write when a mapped file differs from that commit, and rewrites only the selected model's hashes and `syncCommit`. It then reads the record back and checks it. Other records keep their bytes.\n- `scripts/pins.mjs` runs `formal-sources` first. The other steps and their order are unchanged. `scripts/test.mjs` adds the one Node owner.\n- `coverage.json`. Editor and Effects take `syncCommit` 67280592 and the three new hashes. The `limits` of both models now state what the reviewed source changes leave outside the model: source identity checks for Editor, and payload accounting and quota for Effects.\n- The note. It explains why the former gate never read `coverage.json`. It classifies every parent edge of each changed file, and it binds the retained history export and the two audits by SHA-256.\n\nWhat I checked:\n\n- The hashes. At this head, the three refreshed files hash to `b33908e9…`, `c0123652…` and `6e620c33…`, as the note states. `node scripts/check-formal-source-hashes.mjs` in my worktree reports \"8 mapped files in 5 models\" and a match. None of the eight mapped files differs between this head and main 957b2755.\n- The source review. I did not repeat the history audit. I read its categories against the note's claims. Each one keeps the modeled actions, guards and state variables, and each one names the premise that stays outside the model. My approval rests on that audit as the builder recorded it.\n- Scope. The 9 artifacts name the head, and their paths equal `git diff --no-renames --name-only 9d69f807 9f2324dc`.\n- The gate, END 093be55e, on this exact head. `/Users/hughpyle/play/artroom-evidence/2026-11-16-cv1-gate/gate.log` names head 9f2324dc and tree ab5a59a1 with 0 changed files. Pins passed in 18.8 s. Plain 4985 with 1 skipped and Native 1175 with 2 skipped passed, the six scale cases included. The crossing counts were lost 0, stale 3 and no-owner 0, and the last line is \"gate passed\".\n- Cheap checks. The commit-message check passes: 2 checked, 0 flagged. `check-notes` gives 0 warnings, and `git diff --check` is clean. Apart from the hash check, I ran no tests.\n\nArchitecture: sound. The formal source map now has an owner in the gate. A drift fails pins before any test stage, and a refresh is one deliberate command that binds the hashes to a commit.\n\nSecurity: no finding. The guard reads files and writes nothing. The refresh writes only `coverage.json` and refuses uncommitted bytes.\n\nSimplification: no required change.\n\nTwo points on the note, for a later edit:\n\n1. It still says \"Final preflight and the full gate remain UNRUN\", and its table lists both as UNRUN. Gate END 093be55e passed on this head. Record that result and remove the UNRUN rows.\n2. Much of the note is shorthand that a reader outside this lane cannot follow: aliases such as `N`, `A`, `AO`, `F`, `M`, `P`, `MP`, `SC` and `G`, category codes, and line ranges with no surrounding text. The repository's writing rule asks for plain sentences. A short statement would serve better: what the guard does, which hashes changed and why, and where the audit lives.\n\nThis approval authorizes no push, landing or 